INT. LAUNDROMAT - BACK TABLE - NIGHT

Flat fluorescent light. Rows of dryers tumbling. A folding table. A wall of coin machines.

Two siblings, a brother and a sister, fold clothes in silence.

IGNATIUS "IGGY" OYELOWO-BRANDT (30s, grease-stained flannel, hands that don't stay still) folds towels with military corners.

CORINTHIA "CORRIE" OYELOWO-BRANDT (30s, nurse's clogs, tired eyes) folds a child's pajamas into a square the size of her palm.

The machines hum. A dryer thumps.

IGGY
You're doing it wrong.

CORRIE
It's pajamas.

IGGY
There's a way.

CORRIE
Then show me.

He doesn't. She keeps folding. A quarter rattles inside a dryer.

IGGY
How's Mom?

CORRIE
Tuesday she asked who you were.

IGGY
Tuesday I wasn't there.

CORRIE
She asked anyway.

IGGY
What'd you say?

CORRIE
Her son.

IGGY
Good.

Beat. He takes a shirt from the pile.

IGGY (CONT'D)
Corrie, I'm sorry.

CORRIE
For what?

IGGY
I don't know. Everything.

CORRIE
That's not an apology. That's a weather report.

IGGY
I'm trying.

CORRIE
I know you are.

She folds. He folds. A buzzer sounds on a distant machine.

IGGY
I could come Sunday.

CORRIE
You said that last month.

IGGY
I came.

CORRIE
For ten minutes.

IGGY
It was ten good minutes.

CORRIE
It was ten minutes you spent on your phone.

IGGY
It was the shop. A guy needed a quote.

CORRIE
Iggy.

IGGY
Okay.

CORRIE
She's not going to be here forever.

IGGY
I know.

CORRIE
You don't.

He stops. Sets down the shirt. Looks at her for the first time.

IGGY
Corrie, I know.

She meets his eyes. A long beat. Then she hands him the pajamas.

CORRIE
Do it the way you said.

He takes them. Unfolds. Folds again, slowly, creasing each edge with his thumbnail.

IGGY
See?

CORRIE
That's better.

IGGY
It's the same.

CORRIE
It's better.

A dryer finishes and goes quiet. The room hums on. They reach for the next pile together.
